---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `SUP Paddle Technology — Materials, Blade Shapes, and Shaft Stiffness | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Technical guide to SUP paddle design: aluminum vs fiberglass vs carbon shafts, blade shapes for touring vs surfing vs racing, and how paddle weight affects paddling efficiency.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'SUP Paddle Technology Guide',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">SUP Paddle Technology</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Equipment</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">7 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">SUP Paddle Technology Guide</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      The paddle is your primary propulsion tool — its material, weight, blade shape, and shaft stiffness directly
      affect paddling efficiency, endurance, and enjoyment.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>Shaft Materials</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Aluminum:</strong> Most affordable and durable. Standard on entry-level SUPs and included in most bundle kits.
        Heavier (28-32 oz) and less stiff than composites. The weight becomes noticeable on paddles longer than 2-3 miles.
        Aluminum shafts are corrosion-resistant with anodized coating but may bend under extreme stress.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Fiberglass:</strong> Mid-range option with good stiffness-to-weight ratio (18-24 oz). Fiberglass shafts dampen
        vibration better than aluminum, reducing arm fatigue. More expensive than aluminum but significantly lighter. Suitable
        for recreational and intermediate paddlers.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Carbon Fiber:</strong> Premium option — lightest (12-18 oz), stiffest, and best vibration damping. Carbon
        shafts reduce paddling fatigue dramatically on long-distance paddles. The high modulus carbon used in racing paddles
        is stiffer and lighter but more brittle. Standard modulus carbon offers a better durability-to-weight ratio for everyday
        use. Afarer recommends carbon paddles for touring, fitness, and racing paddlers.
      </p>
      <h2>Blade Shapes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Rectangular / Dihedral:</strong> Most common all-around blade shape. The dihedral ridge down the center
          of the blade face improves tracking through the water and reduces flutter. Good for general paddling.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Scoop / Spoon:</strong> Concave blade face that "scoops" water for powerful forward strokes. Preferred by
          touring and racing paddlers for maximum propulsion per stroke. Requires proper technique to use efficiently.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Teardrop / Asymmetric:</strong> Narrower profile with a pointed tip. Reduces blade entry splash and provides
          smooth power throughout the stroke. Common on higher-end adjustable paddles.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Surf / Oval:</strong> Smaller blade surface area for quick, high-cadence strokes. Designed for surfing and
          whitewater where maneuverability matters more than raw power.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Paddle Length</h2>
      <p>
        Correct paddle length is critical for efficient paddling. The general rule: add 8-10 inches to your height for
        recreational paddling. Touring paddlers add 10-12 inches for extended reach. A paddle that is too short forces
        you to bend forward, straining your lower back. A paddle that is too long creates inefficient leverage and
        shoulder fatigue. Adjustable paddles (2-piece or 3-piece telescoping) let you fine-tune length and pack for
        travel.
      </p>
      <h2>Blade Size</h2>
      <p>
        Blade surface area ranges from 65 to 120 square inches. Larger blades (95-120 sq in) provide more power per
        stroke but require more strength. Smaller blades (65-85 sq in) allow higher stroke cadence for cardio training.
        General recommendation: 85-100 sq in for most adult paddlers. Larger paddlers or those seeking power should size
        up; fitness paddlers seeking cardio benefit should size down.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
